Date: Sat, 9 Oct 1993 11:34:21 -0500 (CDT)
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: Forward: Virtual City (tm) Network FAQ 1.0 (fwd)

Thanks, Harry, for forwarding that FAQ.  My comments would have
been inappropriate for Cypherpunks, where I saw it first.  Here,
however, they may have some relevance.

My comments may be completely off-base.  That is, the virtual
city people may be on the brink of supplying what they advertise.
If so, I will print out these comments on nice heavy resume-grade
linen paper and eat them, right after exploring their online
city and using their collaboration and visualization tools,
participating in one of their video conferences, and
sampling their other promised free goodies.

This FAQ (Frequently Asked Questions... does anybody but me doubt
that this material was compiled in response to a flood of questions?)
seems to me to be a perfect example of a rather interesting
phenomenon of our wonderful Reach-Exceeds-GraspNet:  it's lots
easier to make sweeping promises and publicise private fantasies
than to deliver on them.

>The Virtual City(tm) Network is an ambitious Networked Virtual Reality
>Infrastructure

For some definition of "is".

>Using existing software which implements shared,
>interactive virtual spaces

What software is that?  MUDs?  Heh.

>communities in which people may share text, graphics, and multimedia
>in a cooperative real-time environment.

Note the "may".  This is called equivocation.  (i.e. then again, they
may not!)

>be able to make use of cutting edge tools such as network
>conferencing, collaboration & visualization tools, multimedia
>electronic mail, online access to government data, networked library
>catalogs & facilities, electronic books online and Internet-accessible
>public data repositories.

I will translate:  users will be allowed to run 'IRC', 'PINE', 'FTP',
'FTP', 'FTP', and 'FTP'.  Ooh, baby!

>The Virtual City(tm) Network will also be a
>proving ground for privacy technologies such as public key
>cryptography, PGP, and Digital Cash(tm).

Translation:  If we can figure out PGP, we'll let you use it in
some unspecified way.  Oh, and who has trademarked Digital Cash?
If true, at least there's something interesting here.

>DIDN'T YOU RUN AN AD IN _WIRED_ RECENTLY?
>
>NO.  And again, No.

"There's no way we could afford that!"

>M. Strata Rose, longtime net.lurker and sometime visionary

Heh.

>who has been developing the concept since roughly June 1992.  I started
>serious feasibility exploration in December of '92

Oops, slipped into first person there.  Oh well.  Rule of thumb:
when self-publishing dimly-seen visions, always use the royal 'we'.

  [jun '92: summer vacation after Junior Year.  dec '92: christmas break]

> on a timeline which formally started in May '93.

Following graduation, in a tough job market.

>Obviously there is a limit to the extent to which I can comment,
>as both of our offerings seem to be in a pre-release state.

Heh.

>The Virtual City (tm) Metropolitan Transit Authority, VCMTA,
>is being designed concurrently as an object transport model between
>instantiations of various Virtual City (tm) Sites.  We will be
>offering templates for instantiations of our city model to be used by
>communities and organizations, with a true distributed model allowing
>users and information to flow freely throughout the Virtual City (tm)
>Network.

Creative Acronyms (tm) aside, this is impenetrable.  Hopefully they
(he) have (has) a clearer vision than this!

>We are attempting to build an expandable, scalable piece of Internet
>infrastructure that will support a rich model of growth and
>self-determination as well as support research on virtual communities
>and cyberspaces in general.

And what will it provide that the _current_ expandable, scalable
Internet infrastructure doesn't?

>RFC's and API's

More words.  Good.

>In the Virtual City(tm) Network, the line between the real and the
>virtual has the inherent ability to be deliberately blurred.

A Sociology 101 paper that your TA thought was hip doesn't a
business plan make.

>I hope to feature several space station designs

Hope does spring eternal!  Kind of invigorating.

>We are currently using LambdaMOO

but since we haven't implemented anything or done any concrete
design, we're free to chuck that in favor of AlphaSMURF if we
like.  Flexibility, that's the key!

>Xerox PARC is working on a project called "AstroVR"

which we hope someday to talk to them about licensing (as soon
as the stationery is ready).

>This is just the beginning.

Hee.

>Our model is very similar to the one which NCSA
>developed with Mosaic.

In fact, since we'll be grabbing MOSAIC as our user interface, it's identical!

>By the way, if you
>register an object for public use and it passes the Virtual City (tm)
>Architectural Board (no Trojan horses or duds, please!), it no longer
>counts against your quota.  Keen, eh?

Oh Joy!  Finally, an organization that will let me develop their
software for them without charging me!

>Cultural attractions such as art galleries, museums, and music
>halls are placed throughout the Virtual City(tm) Network

They are?  Can I see?

>go out and connect to a public key server and get yourself a key

Bzzzt.  Oh well -- hopefully this guy understands how SOME of the
technologies he's intending to merge actually work.  As was pointed
out on Cypherpunks (by Hal I think), this ain't the way it works!

>We are planning a Virtual New Year's Party at the very least, even if
>all net services are not on-line yet.

Translation:  we may have a minimally-functional MUD ready, from
which we (sic) will assess the interest level and then scuttle the
project.  Damn apathetic net.jerks.

>WELL, DO YOU NEED ANY HELP?  HOW DO I GET INVOLVED?

Heh.

> Our schedule has been accelerated fairly rapidly 

"it's hard to save money as a college student"

>"virtual-citizens@virtual.net"

Now, despite this message, I do like to see people trying things,
and will subscribe to this list.  These people's unfamiliarity
with the technology, the difficulty of recruiting volunteer labor
for J.Random.Net-lurker's vision, feauturitis, and a lack of any
clear target set of facilities and market make me think that this
will remain in possibility-space rather than reality-space.  I
hope I'm wrong and that the natural tendency of people to mistake
dreams for impending realities can in some instances change the
world.

I just wish that people would get their ordering straight:
get something working, _then_ talk about it.  And keep
plans, goals, and dreams to less than half of a project's
public persona.  Helps credibility immensely.

derek
look for my forthcoming FAQ on an underground crypto-anarchic community/
cryonic suspension facility.  Research labs, brain implants, the works!


