From: cappello@cs.ucsb.edu (Peter Cappello)
Subject: GUNS, SOC: Burglary and countermeasures

First, thank you, dV/dt, for a temperate reply.

>I would think it obvious that killing them is not the primary  
>objective -- the use (or, more properly, the threat) of deadly force  
>is to keep them in place for the cops to come get them.

Understood.

>If they sit quietly and wait to be arrested, then there is clearly  
>no need to shoot them.

dV/dt, let's say he politely declines your offer for him to sit and  
wait to be arrested.  He, instead, quietly gets up, and walks away  
from you, towards the door with your VCR in his arms.

Are you prepared to shoot him with the intention of killing him?   
Forget about conventional morality.  Forget about the law.  What is  
best for you?  If you kill him, and the legal authorities clear you  
of any wrongdoing, is that the end of it?  His kin will know you shot  
him in cold blood for stealing your VCR.  They'll know where you  
live.  Maybe you and your loved ones will _not_ be killed (via  
"Murder, Inc.") in retaliation.  Then again ... Would killing a  
burglar in cold blood over a VCR be worth the risk to yourself and  
your loved ones?  (In a crypto-anarchy scenario, retaliation seems  
even more likely.)  Or will you pre-empt his kin by having them all  
killed first?  Either way, you will have killed someone.  Doing so  
has lifelong consequences.  You will be engaged in a feud, a battle  
for your life, for the rest of your life, however long. Perhaps your  
side will "win."  When will you know you have won?  When will you  
know that someone isn't stalking you?  What is the total cost to your  
life?  Is a life and death feud your chosen lifestyle?  There might  
be considerably less time for play or productive work.  


Of course, if many people used deadly force against burglars who do  
not sit when told, then many burglars would cease these acts.  This  
seems like a public good.  However, those burglars that continued  
would, as a matter of course, kill anyone who happened to be home  
("and ask questions later").  It's difficult (for me, at least) to  
assess a priori the net benefit to society.  As the joke goes (see  
Tim Slagle's comedy routine), if the punishment for drug use is  
greater than that for rape and murder, the lesson learned is to rape  
and murder the cop arresting you for drug use.

I believe that "Let the punishment fit the crime." is a Schelling  
point of human relations.  While determining "fit" is a subjective  
matter, major deviations, one way or the other, have downsides.  I  
think that you are well served by fully exploring them.

I do not intend to sound certain about what is best; I'm not.   
Killing in cold blood over a VCR _may_ not be the best strategy for  
someone intending to live forever.  Other reactions or proactions may  
serve.  Insure yourself against theft.  Let the insurance pros help  
you with your security system.  Let them pursue your burglar, if they  
care to; he's stolen from them.  Let specialization of labor work for  
you. 


[It would be nice for a technology to develop whereby your VCR would  
self destruct, if not instructed by its proper owner (a crypto  
application?).  I, for one, also would like to see a technology  
developed for safely incapacitating a person without killing them.]

If you want to experience a PPL extropia, then survival is primary.   
Stay cool.  Whatever you choose to do, dV/dt, please do it with your  
eyes open.

-Pete

