Subject: Its all out there, somewhere

So, having pondered the imponderables about Blacknet Dossiers, I put
the stuff in the back of my mind.

Today, I was going through some of the stuff on the Clarinet
"Electronic Science Fiction" CD-ROM that I mentioned earlier on this
list. One of the things in it is a large collection of old SF-LOVERS
digests. SF-LOVERS, which may or may not still exist (I don't follow
these things) was around for a very long time, and I vaguely
remembered having posted something at some point to it, so I did a
search, and I came up with something I wrote seven years ago. Yowza!
The thing that shocked me the most is that my writing back then wasn't
that bad -- not the perfection I produce now, of course, but
reasonable. By bizarre coincidence, I was arguing with Brad Templeton,
the man who went on to found Clarinet and who put together the very
CD-ROM in question! Very odd.

Anyway, the point is, I suspect everything I've written to the net is
out there somewhere. How very very strange, and at the same time
wonderful. In a century, there is actually a hope that I can get a
glimpse of what I was once like, through my own writing. There is also
the threat that our race will get a memory that never forgets, even
things that we might want it to. Well, it makes little difference --
its going to happen if we want it or not.

What I'm really interested in doing at some point is seeing if I can
get copies of some of my comments on the presidential campaign of a
decade ago that I made on a long gone BBS before I became a
libertarian and was simply a muzzy headed left winger. I have a vague
feeling that they still exist...

